 "gaps": "1) The Faour thesis itself was not retrieved. The paper cites it as https://reductions.network/papers/faour_extending_reductions_network_a_survey_of_parameterized_complexity. It was not needed for the enumeration - the parameterized vertex list and its W-classes were taken from the live data repository, which is what the site actually renders - but no independent cross-check of the 29 names against the thesis text was performed. 2) The `# completeness` field records set membership and hardness as separate tokens. 'W[1]'+'W[1]-hard' is read here as W[1]-complete. For the 11 problems tagged 'W[2]'+'W[1]-hard', the repo asserts membership in W[2] but only W[1]-hardness, NOT W[2]-hardness; several of these are W[2]-complete in the literature, but that stronger claim is not made by this source and is not asserted here. `completeness_raw` preserves the exact tokens. 3) Six classic entries are unfilled stubs in the repo (instances/solutions/citations literally 'todo', empty completeness): Chinese Postman, Directed Rural Postman, Undirected Rural Postman, Minimal Maximal Matching, s-t-Max Cut, and (partially) several others with empty completeness - Edge Dominating Set, Longest Common Subsequence, Not-All-Equal 4SAT, Planar Vertex Cover, Vertex Cover on Cubic Graph, Maximum Cut(classic, has citation). They are genuine vertices of the network but the repo establishes no complexity value for them. 4) Counts differ slightly from the ~57/~23/~29 figures in the tasking: classic is 58 (not 57). Figure 1 renders 57 legible labels because two distinct vertices both abbreviate to 'MLST' (Maximum Leaf Spanning Tree and Minimum Leaf Spanning Tree) and 'EXACT COVER' (EC) is drawn occluded behind the '3-Dim Matching' node; GRAPH COLORING is present in the repo but was not located in the figure. The repo listing (58) is taken as authoritative over the figure. 5) Vertex counts are as of commit 8089fb4f3b0d674550ce00449d41533b0d7e3404 (2025-12-13); the live site may have drifted since."
